# Basics { .heading-no-number }

---

## Functions

Functions are declared with `fun`. The body is indented; no braces needed.

```koka
fun square( x : int ) : int
  x * x

fun example-square()
  println(square(7).show)
```

Type annotations are optional -- Koka infers them. Hover over identifiers
in VS Code to see inferred types.

---

## Dot Notation

`x.f(y)` is sugar for `f(x, y)`. This lets you chain calls naturally:

```koka
fun example-dot()
  "hello world".to-upper.println
```

Any function can be a "method" on its first argument -- no class system required.
This also means you can extend any type by writing a new function.

---

## Trailing Lambdas

A function's last argument can be written as an indented block after the call:

```koka
fun example-trailing()
  [1, 2, 3].map fn(x)
    x * x
```

If the lambda takes no arguments, just use braces:

```koka
fun example-repeat()
  repeat(3)
    println("hello!")
```

This is how `while`, `repeat`, and other control flow works -- they are
ordinary functions, not special syntax.

---

## Braces as Suspension

An expression in parentheses is evaluated _before_ the call. An expression
in braces is _suspended_ -- it may be evaluated zero, one, or many times:

```unchecked
while { i < 10 }    // condition re-evaluated each iteration
  println("hello")
```

This is consistent: `while` is a regular function taking two suspended arguments.

---

## The `with` Statement

`with` turns a trailing-lambda call into a scoping construct. Instead of:

```unchecked
handle-errors( fn()
  do-work()
)
```

Write:

```unchecked
with handle-errors
do-work()
```

Everything after `with` becomes the function body passed to the handler.
This is the key syntactic mechanism for effect handlers.

---

## Optional and Named Parameters

```koka
fun greet( name : string, greeting : string = "Hello" )
  println(greeting ++ ", " ++ name ++ "!")

fun example-greet()
  greet("World")
  greet("World", greeting = "Hi")
```

---

## Local Mutable Variables

`var` declares a locally mutable variable. Its state cannot escape:

```koka
fun sum-list( xs : list<int> ) : int
  var total := 0
  xs.foreach fn(x)
    total := total + x
  total
```

The inferred type is `total int` -- no state effect leaks out.
Koka automatically discharges local state at the function boundary.

